{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/performance.md at exact a9319499110a9cb257855673364e032ab58d7354 on request/projection-cost, under promise git:sha1…62bf4cf on request git:sha1…5616ac4\n\nRepublished after the changes-requested verdict 87c959bf. What moved, and what\nthe review asked for:\n\n(a) The forward-citation guard is gone, and with it its test. The review was\nright and I verified it before acting: w.forward was set only where the walk\nrecursed past the receipt's index, while the read the memo depends on — the\nstaleness of a basis the walk decides not to enter, at the continue in\ncausesSettledAtReceipt — happens first. The guard fired only where the\nunsoundness would already have been visible, so it read as a proof and was not\none. The memo is now unconditional, and the three facts that make the reuse exact\nare stated at the memo: the narrowed plan and the dates come from the receipt and\nthe scope and do not change inside a pass; the walk reads staleness only of the\nreceipt's own basis closure, which the kernel's refusal of a rests_on naming an\nevent the log does not yet hold places at or below the receipt's index; and\nclosure() visits records in ascending order, so those values are final when the\nfirst successor asks.\n\n(b) The mutation table is corrected, and the two comments that claimed the\ninterval walk depends on ascending query order now say what the review found:\ncorrectness does not, because postReceiptAccounting bounds every answer with the\nsuccessor that asked, and only the cost does.\n\n(c) The retired filter in liveArtifactIndex now has a red proof.\nTestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact asserts that a protected\nsibling survives a retired later artifact at its own path and yields to a live\none; removing the filter turns it red. It also passes against unmodified main, so\nit pins behaviour that already held.\n\nConditions met at this exact head.\n\n1. Order of magnitude on an artifact-heavy log. Read-only fold and projection of\na frozen copy of the /Users/hughpyle/play/dap workroom (7,029 events, 5,091\nartifacts, merge receipts present): 18.10 s before, 2.06 s and 2.07 s after. This\nrepository's own log (23,945 events, 6,610 artifacts, 2,697 commitments): 21.70 s\nbefore, 13.03 s after. Same machine, runs taken one after the other, frozen\ncopies so no concurrent actor moved the head. The two differ because they spend\ntheir time in different halves: the artifact-heavy log spent it in the\nprojection, which is what a reader waits for after every act; this repository\nspends it in the fold, at one complete staleness pass per merge receipt admitted,\nwhich a resident pays when it starts.\n\n2. Every projection is unchanged. The complete projection and vocabulary of both\nlogs, serialized as JSON, are byte-identical before and after: 25,233,705 bytes\nfor the artifact-heavy log and 52 MB for this one. The reviewer also built an\nindependent corpus and found 183,168 bytes byte-identical between this head's\nparent and the previous head, with the checkpoint answering 78 times and 36 memo\nhits, and separately showed memo-on equals memo-off.\n\n3. pathCovers allocates nothing (testing.AllocsPerRun, 0) and its boundaries are\nnamed: exact match, directory above, trailing slash on either side, a sibling\nsharing a prefix without a separator, and a path below its predecessor.\n\n4. No memo outlives the fact it caches. The interval walks and the live-artifact\nindex are built for one projection and dropped with it, and\nTestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold appends a retirement after a\nprojection and shows the next projection reflects it.\n\n5. Named unit tests cover each optimized path, and every new guard now has a\nmutation that turns a named test red:\n\n  pathCovers separator: TestSuccessionFollowsOnlyACoveringSuccessor,\n    TestPathCoversBoundariesWithoutAllocating\n  empty-interval fast path widened by one:\n    TestMergeLeftLivePostFrontierRemainsUnaccountedUntilNextReceipt,\n    TestPostReceiptAccountingStopsAtTheSuccessor,\n    TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  bound each answer by its own successor: TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  resume the interval walk rather than restart it: TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  changed-path filter in the interval walk:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsBoundsClaimsAndPostFrontierAccounting,\n    TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  live subset of the counted interval: TestPostReceiptAccountingSeparatesCountedFromOwed,\n    TestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold\n  post-frontier bound on the sibling index:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n  the receipt's own successor is not a later artifact:\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact,\n    TestReceiptProtectionBeforeAtAndAfterClosure\n  retired artifacts are not live replacements: TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n  per-target supersession index: TestSupersedingARequestThatHoldsAnApprovedHead,\n    TestAnExplicitAbandonmentOutranksAQualifyingTransfer,\n    TestSupersessionIndexHoldsEachTargetInLogOrder\n  commitment protection is read at all:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n\nTwo mutations are green, and both are stated rather than claimed. Removing the\nreceipt memo entirely changes no answer, which is the point: it changes speed.\nReading commitment protection with an empty staleness set also changes no answer,\nand the reason is structural — the active-status set admits every unfinished\nstatus including \"stale\", so staleness cannot move a row out of it. The red proof\nabove is for protection being read at all, which is what the change is; the\nprevious head's table labelled that line as if it proved the staleness argument,\nand this is the correction the review asked for.\n\n6. docs/reference/performance.md records the measurement, both corpora shapes,\nthe four repeated reads, and what the numbers do not establish.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean and go test -count=1 ./... green at this exact head, exit 0\nchecked directly rather than through a pipeline, 37 packages, no failures,\n40-minute timeout.\n\nArchitecture: unchanged from the previous head and confirmed by the review. The\nchange is inside the Workroom application profile's fold and the projection it\nalready produced; no layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md moves,\nwhich is why that page is not in this artifact set. The reviewer tested the claim\nthat matters — that the exact projection bytes a cache is keyed on do not move,\nso no fold version bump is owed — on a corpus built to make them move.","body":{"commit":"a9319499110a9cb257855673364e032ab58d7354","path":"docs/reference/performance.md"}}